        <Title>Before inserting Pel</Title>
        <Text>A Solitant priest hears the word ‘white’ and thinks of the pure white glow of the god named Solraheen. Hrkan folk hear ‘white’ and think of the plain and stainless robes of Hrkan Lordship. Outland pokes hear ‘white’ and think of sparkling salt pans, or sun-bleached bone. As it is with white, so it is with words, they mean different things to different people. It is some wonder then, that Solraheen would choose to use such an equivocal thing as a word to create the world. He dwelt in the void, spoke one word, the world emerged, and people haven’t stopped arguing about the meaning of it all ever since. 
One might be led to believe that He chose to create with a word because He wanted to play with what He meant. But this is false, or so the Solitants claim. Words are not equivocal, they are fixed. Or rather, they used to be.
The word ‘art’, for example, originally had a clear meaning. Back when Solraheen walked the earth an artwork was nothing less than a divine message given physical form. Then evil came into the world, twisted the tongues of men, and now the heresy of art as a means of personal expression pollutes the minds of many.
An old Solitant sermon titled ‘Art as Exegetical Instrument’ extracts the truth of the matter through interrogation:
What is art?
Who is an artist?
Who isn’t?
A painter, a sculptor, these are artists, yes, that’s reasonable to say, but what about a tax collector? Is he an artist too if he plumbs the depths of greed? Is a sheep-shearer plying her shears an artist if she’s sufficiently clever about it? What about a farmer? Give a young man a shovel, a field of dirt, friends to impress, and what does he do? The priest leaves the derivation of the answer as an exercise to the audience. For the prude, the answer is a penis. The average Jandri boy will find it funny to dig a big old ugly in the dirt. He laughs because it doubles as a drainage ditch, just like the real thing. To place such degeneracy in the same category as the cloud of colored windows which open up the ceiling of the Gambagall—well! That is an affront to the sacred.
Two days ago, Vigram heard a variation of this sermon. He didn’t normally go into the temple, because that meant he’d have to listen to someone else speak, but he’d gone, and so young Mandrameen was forced to deal with the consequences.
Burly, broad-faced Vigram, with his gleaming scalp and belly beard. He smacked his rich breakfast table with a fat hand. 
“The Solitants are right!” Vigram roared. “If everyone is an artist, then the word is as useful as an unmarked weight!” 
Vigram clutched his bearded jaw and winced. The food on his table did not accommodate his toothache. This was his leech farm, and so every dish’s principal ingredient was some variation of the Meskalimi leech. This particular leech was a prized delicacy throughout the Hrkan world as its essence was said to cure infertility, and somehow, fertility as well. The meat, if it can be called meat, was quite sweet. It was also chewy. Every bite forced Vigram’s tongue into acrobatics. Two bites in, it slipped, and the pain had him talking out the side of his cheek in an odd drawl. It hurt to hear the man speak, in more ways than one.
“Why not choose a different word?” Mandri suggested. 
“Because words must have walls,” Vigram said as he pushed his plate away. “The true artist, I say this, the true artist is a divine instrument. They must be kept apart from the Self-addled horde.”
Mandri chowed down on the cold, crumbled chunks of what he supposed was boiled leech. It was quite bitter on account of the overpowering sauce, but actively trying to suppress a gag helped keep him from speaking his mind.
“You know,” Vigram said, getting excited, “the Old Jandri word for ‘artist’ is lost to time, but there is a word in what’s left of that language fit for the task—‘bursheg’. It means something like ‘fertile ground’, but in the Solitant tradition it means ‘true artist’.”
“Interesting,” Mandri lied.
“I’ll tell you what’s interesting,” Vigram said, lifting his thick brows. “The old witch who gave that sermon, she said that art has nothing to do with what a person makes. Have you ever heard that? I didn’t understand it at first, but you know, it’s true. True artistry, consider this, true artistry is a state of mind. Some of the greatest artists never made anything at all.”
Mandri stuffed his mouth with a breadroll in a futile attempt to stifle himself. “I was under the impression,” he said as he worked the roll into his cheek, “that an artist makes art.”
Vigram frowned and tutted. “Nobody makes anything,” he insisted. “We can only ever rearrange. When you paint, you push pigment around. How is that any different than my footprints rearranging sand? No, either everyone is an artist, or, and this is my claim, an artist is a certain kind of person. A certain kind of mind. You know, too many big ideas, too many shallow excuses, that sort of thing. It’s a mind marked by excess—not abundance—excess.”
“Don’t look at me,” Mandri said.
Vigram smiled. The fat swerve of his bottom lip was a baited hook. “So exaggerated is the artist’s sense of self that it becomes impossible to describe them without resorting to sarcasm.”
“You’re still looking at me.”
Vigram’s smile swerved into a bully’s smirk. “There is no one else here.” 
Two slaves stood at attention by the kitchen’s double-swing doors. Vigram’s blindness to their existence was cruel, but this did not dismantle his point, as Mandri did think highly of himself.
Why shouldn’t he?
When he was ten, he could paint a portrait in oils that rivaled the old Jeketian masters. These weren’t polish-jobs where the paint was thin and every hair was rendered into being like he’d lost the plot of reality. By his hand, a single stroke of umber was enough to define the chestnut skin, the high-arched cheek, the skull underneath, the light, the shadow, even the mood of the piece. Warms and cools in one single stroke! With twenty-four, he could find a vivid likeness. There might be a few extra nibbles to make a beauty’s eyes glisten and her cleavage heave, but nobody counted those. Well, Vigram counted them. Lascivious men counted them. Probably, every man counted them. Some women, presumably.
And like any prideful artist, Mandri would strut around the grand Hrkan galleries and make clear his contempt for his contemporaries. He’d stand in front of a painting and say, “This piece really moves me,” and he’d make a show of walking away. All must bear witness to his mop of black hair as it flounced itself in time with his skinny, stiff-backed step. Or he’d say, “I weep at every stroke,” and he’d flash a knowing smile. Back in those days, he’d say these things directly to a fellow artist’s face if he could get to it. He never thought of it as bullying because artists, like himself, were safe targets for playful derision; it wasn’t the person he was making fun of, it was their failed attempts to express themselves, and those were completely different things. 
He’d never go after the person.
No, never.
Artists were the best people. They needed tough skin, unearned confidence, and a flair for the dramatic. Without any of that, an artist expires in obscurity. Then there is the servility. Above all, an artist must be servile to the hand that feeds. And look at that, Vigram had a lot of food at his table.
“Vigram,” Mandri said confidently, “you are an idiot.”
Vigram choked on a wad of smushed up leech. He coughed, pounded his chest, then coughed again. Once recovered, he glared at Mandri with protuberant eyes, oblivious to the brown specks of food caught in his long beard. “In whose house do we sit?!”
“Yours, of course.” Mandri said as he tried to muster a disarming smile. “But when it comes to these things, when it comes to matters of art, I am correct. If I am not correct, then you certainly aren’t.”
“Why? Because I like some painters who aren’t you?”
“I don’t paint anymore, so I don’t care who you like.”
“If you’re not a painter, then why do I pay you for lessons?”
“These lessons are a charity.”
“Mandri, I pay you!”
“Even so.”
Leech juice dripped down the corner of Vigram’s lip, accenting his frown. He wiped it away, then broke into a jolly smile. “Hah!” he exclaimed, and he stabbed his clam shell into a bowl of fermented leech. So much lard was smashed into those leeches that the gray paste could grease a wheel. Vigram’s outstretched tongue batted at the stringy slop before he slumped it all down his gullet. He patted his fat lips dry with his fifth napkin, then threw that napkin onto the floor.
“We both know,” he said, “that you only come here for the money.”
“Then we are both mistaken.”
Vigram rolled his eyes and set his leech lard mush aside. “Listen,” he said. He put on his business face. It looked something like a spooked coconut. “You have a gift. I should say that I believe in your talent. You should believe me when I say that success is there for the taking. I know you don’t want to debase yourself by doing what it takes to succeed, but the money is easy. It is sitting there for the taking. People want to give it to you.” 
Mandri settled into his chair because he knew what was coming next: Vigram’s life story. It always came out as a sort of instructional, as if Mandri could do it, too.
The first step in the accumulation of wealth was often left out because not everyone could easily murder their father to inherit a sizable sum. That being said, four ounces of silver would do.
Next, loan those four ounces out to a leech farm. Why a leech farm? Beyond the blunt symbolism, there’s not much of a reason. It is important to remember that not every leech farm is the same. Look for a farm that supplies a Solitant temple with leeches for their bloodletting rituals. That’s a surefire bet because temples are always the last thing the mob will torch in lean times. When the lean times hit especially hard, when the only bread available is made from fern-roots, the leech farm will feel the squeeze. They’ll ask for leniency on the interest rate. Do not budge. Take over the farm. The leechmen might accuse you of committing usury against Solraheen Himself, because in some small way you are, but Solraheen is dead and so what does it matter? Get together with a priest and pray about it around a jug of beer, flash him a slip of silver, and the bleary shine will blind him to all the world’s iniquity.
With the farm in hand, begin to lend smaller amounts over shorter terms to farmers who need emergency loans to pay their taxes after droughts or infestations. Split these debts up and sell them off in Meskalim’s bond market. There’s not much money in Meskalim itself, but it has a busy port and that means access to all the important markets of the Hrkan world.
From there, it’s a simple matter of reducing your eyes to tiny pinpricks, ridding yourself of limbs, segmenting the body, and now that you’ve become a leech entirely, you may attach firmly the to the continent’s rump and drink your fill. Anyone who tries to shake you off before you’ve finished becomes your slave. You pick out the salvageable fools for your own use, and now you’ve got a brood of leeches pouring over lists of sums credited and debts owed. So long as you maintain a layer of abstraction between these creatures and your henchmen, it’s all self-sustaining.
And that’s it.
Now you have a comfortable life.
Idle hours.
“No debasement necessary,” Vigram added before licking his plump fingers clean of sun-dried leech oil. This oil was a true delicacy, squeezed as it was from the salivary glands of leeches aged twenty years or more. Leeches that big are more devourers than bloodsuckers, so they’re much more expensive to feed.
“Now,” Vigram said. “You know all about the making of a painting. That’s why I respect you. I know all about the making of money. At a certain point you have to join them up, because the world doesn’t work how it should. The world works how it works. You have to make a living. Success, Mandri, is a very sweet thing.”
He clapped his meaty hands, prompting one of the nameless slaves to come and take his plates. Once done, he stood up. His chair protested the sudden shift of his massive weight with a terrible squeak.
“We should begin,” he said.
Mandri nodded, then wiped his own mouth and excused himself from the table. He took his time, because he wasn’t looking forward to beginning anything. But, he had to. He needed the money.  He followed Vigram out to the vine-trellised, brown-brick patio which offered a grand view of Vigram’s leech lakes.
Twelve massive man-made leech lakes tiled Vigram’s large estate, each a perfect square. The rim of the nearest squares were lined with roofless stalls, with each stall penning a live animal into the shallows. Goats, horses, cows; any animal larger than a large dog would do. They were each of them slumped onto support beams. Their snouts were tied into bags of intoxicating freeweed smoke. This was not an act of beneficence to inure them to the pain, it was Vigram’s way of keeping his peaceful country air free from their wild moaning.
The rest of the estate was mostly flies and marshgrass. The sky was cloudy with the kind of tall, mountainous clouds that swallowed landscape paintings whole, the kind that cast broad shadows down upon the world to pull the viewer through the distance, as they pulled Mandri now, all the way out to the sparkling blue line on the horizon which was the sea.
Vigram set himself to work in setting up an easel which he claimed to have built himself. Slaves cut and planed the lumber, brewed the glue, hammered the metal fittings, but Vigram’s mighty mind designed it.
“Are you sure you want to do this here?” Mandri asked.
“Why not here?” said Vigram.
Mandri eyed the distressed animals in the lakes below. Shaved down to the pink skin like criminals, they’d been reduced to bags of blood. Leechmen waded in the waters between the stalls so that they could trowel off the largest leeches and toss them into grass baskets. Those leeches which weren’t engaged in the constant struggle for the cleanest spot of flesh were busy rippling the water’s surface with their mating. It was a bit of an orgy down there. If only those foul creatures knew that their bliss would end with their bellies being split-open, fried, and served on a plate.
Mandri felt his stomach turn. On came a pang of guilt, because all those goats, horses, and cows were suffering in leech paradise on account of him. He’d eaten more than his fill of Meskalimi leech. Who was he to judge Vigram? What right did he have to feel compassion for those animals when his own stomach was full of leech?
“Well,” Mandri said as he eyed the patio’s trellis. “These vines aren’t yet thick enough for a solid shadow. A dappled light will fall upon the sitter. It’s going to make things difficult.”
He moved into the dappled shade to demonstrate the effect on his own skin.
“My dear Mandriiii,” Vigram said, his pained drawl rolling heavily. “The patterns of light and shadow are a magical vesture.”
“Well,” Mandri pondered, “I might suggest that you worry about ‘magical vestures’ when you’re skilled enough to make a face look like a face.”
“No,” Vigram said firmly, long beard wiggling. “I won’t have it any other way. A magical vesture for a magical subject.”
As if bidden from the heavens by Vigram’s words, a woman joined them on the patio. Her clean skin and sun-kissed cheeks evinced her comely nature, and more, she was Mandri’s age. And even more, her waist was wide enough to belong to a fertility goddess. Many a man would leap at the chance, but Mandri averted his eyes. He had never seen her before, and though she was the sitter for Vigram’s portrait, she was not what he was here for.
“Isn’t she a subject fit for holy oils?” Vigram asked, squeezing out a suggestive smile.
“Undoubtedly,” Mandri said. His eyes flicked again to the girl, without intention.
“This is Lora,” Vigram announced. “My niece.”
“You’re niece?!” Mandri blurted. “You don’t have any family.”
“I lied.”
“You lied about having a niece?”
Vigram shook his head with short little shakes. “Move past it, Mandri. This is Lora. She’s visiting. I’ve told her all about you.”
“Nice to meet you, Mandri,” Lora said. Her voice was soft and clear. She extended her arm in greeting. Around her wrist, a thin gold chain choked the softest flesh that Mandri had ever seen. Indeed, her short frame carried such a clean distribution of fat that it seemed she was boneless, an articulated pillow. That she stood upright seemed impossible. How? The strength of the perfection of the feminine, perhaps, or else her tendons were attached to the giddiness of men. Her unworked hand melted around his own knobby claw.
Mandri smiled, pulled back, and averted his eyes. Vigram stared at the girl like he was about to stamp a return on interest. Seeing that, Mandri’s mind was made up: there was no possible world in which Vigram and Lora were related. Why they were pretending was a mystery.
“He seems disturbed,” Lora said.
“By your beauty,” Vigram assured. “Of that, I have no doubt.”
Mandri smiled through a mask of amicability, then let his nervous gaze flee out into the leech lakes. He stared into the wild eye of an old yellow headed ox that was stuck in the nearest stall of the nearest lake. The creature stared back at him. For some god-bothering reason it saw a ray of hope in his unfamiliar face. It mooed, bucked against its restraints, then mooed again. It was asking for help.
“White light!” Vigram roared as he rushed to the edge of the patio. “Quiet that animal!”
The leechmen below scurried into action.
“And you,” Vigram said to Mandri, “help Lora onto her stool. I’ll ready the canvas and paint.”
Mandri sighed a sigh for the old ox, and privately resolved to never eat a Meskalimi leech again. Then he did what he was told helped Lora up onto a high-legged stool.
Lora let her fingers linger on his arm as he pulled away.
“Stop distracting my muse,” Vigram teased.
Again, Mandri did as he was told and took up his post behind the old man.
Vigram snatched up a stick of soft charcoal and put down his first exploratory marks. This was always the most difficult phase. Every work of art must be built on a firm foundation; get it wrong and everything that comes after will be wrong as well. Unfortunately for Vigram, he had that all too common beginner’s habit of refusing to use his god damned brain. He started drawing wherever his hand happened to land, and whatever happened next was up to Solraheen. The end result was that Lora’s head was half the size it needed to be. It was also too left-of-center. There’d be an awkward expanse behind her flower-studded curls.
“Hold on,” said Mandri. “You’re moving too fast. Don’t think of this as a portrait—not yet. All we have right now is an oval, a rectangular canvas, and where that oval sits on that canvas. All you’re doing is putting an oval on a rectangle.”
“Right.”
“So, think about, where’s the best place to put that oval? How big should it be? Remember, you have the whole canvas there.”
“Yes. Yes, you’re right.”
Vigram rubbed the marks away with his palm.
He stepped back.
He studied Lora.
He stepped again to the canvas, then scratched another black oval exactly where the first had been.
“Is that better?” he asked with all the awareness of a potted plant. “You think it needs to be bigger?” He drew the oval again, but only slightly larger. From then on, every other mark he made was accompanied by a look back to Mandri for reassurance. 
“Don’t look at me,” said Mandri, frustrated. “Look at her.”
“But I need to know if the marks I make are correct.”
“They’re never correct.”
There was a glimmer of understanding in Vigram’s eye, but just as soon as it came it faded. “If the mark is always wrong, then how could I ever move past it? I’ll be redrawing the same line over and over again.”
“You move on when you can’t stand to try anymore.”
“That sounds miserable.”
“It is,” Mandri said, feeling understood. “You should be defeated by the time you’re finished. In fact, you should never feel good about what you’re doing, because that means you’re not trying hard enough. Now, finish…finish the basic shapes, at least.”
“Alright.”
Vigram ‘finished’ the ‘basic shapes’. He looked to Mandri for final approval.
“It’s your paint,” Mandri said with a shrug. “But you need to understand the importance of the drawing. You’re always drawing. Even when painting, you’re drawing.”
“I know,” Vigram whined. “You talk about it all the time.”
Vigram made to redraw the too-large chin, but was interrupted by a loud, mournful ox bellow. It had come from same yellow-headed ox that had asked for Mandri’s help. 
Vigram shook his fist. “Damn it!” he cried.
“Oh, please,” Lora sighed. “Focus. Let your boys take care of the beast. It’s their job.”
“If they were doing their job, I wouldn’t have heard that noise!”
“Please,” Lora repeated. “Focus.”
Vigram closed his eyes and took a deep breath. When he next looked upon his canvas, he was resolved. “I’m done drawing. I’m here to paint!”
He tossed his charcoal over his shoulder and picked up his favorite brush. He dipped it into a bluish shade of bronze, then threw down the first splotches of color in a flurry. It was an affront to the senses, and like a notary to an execution, Mandri was getting paid to witness it. And Sol’s light, that bentberry fiber canvas was innocent, expensive, too, a weave coveted for its depth of feeling, for how it made the undertones blush. Assaulted by Vigram’s hand, those tones were bruised and near to bleeding, though he didn’t quite attack the painting; it was more of a confused slapping around.
Mandri had to look away.
He glanced in Lora’s direction.
The moment his eyes wandered over her exposed shoulder, her smile perked up. 
“Mandri, stop flirting with my neice.”
“I am not flirting,” Mandri claimed. “I’m checking proportions.”
“Yes, and how are her proportions?”
The old goat’s eyebrows jumped suggestively. Lora squirmed in her seat, blushing in feigned embarrassment. The yellow headed ox weighed in with an oxen roar.
“MRRRRRUUUUUUUUUUUUU!”
“That’s it!” Vigram shouted. Below, in the leech lake, two leechmen tried to force the ox’s snout into a bag of freeweed smoke. “That’s IT!”
Vigram rushed to the edge of the patio, apparently expecting the display of his rage would solve the situation, but no, the yellow-headed ox bucked both leechmen off, broke its constraints, then jumped its rail. The beast tore through the leech lake, kicking up waves of confused, copulating leeches. “Stop that ox!” Vigram shouted as he pointed with his loaded brush. “There’s a silver-string of leeches attached to that beast! If you don’t—”
“Please!” Lora moaned from her seat. “You’re rich enough already. You don’t need the money from another bundle of leeches. Let it go!”
“I can’t.”
“Vigram,” Lora said sharply. “Let. It. Go.”
Mandri wondered what kind of niece would first-name her uncle like that. Order him around? What kind of Vigram could console his rage with a frustrated headshake?
“Don’t ask me to let go of my principles,” Vigram replied. “That I cannot do.”
He marched back towards the canvas and set his brush down on the palette, not caring that it rolled into a swatch of blue. Ever the man of action, he marched down to the leech lakes, where he ordered five other men to take action. He screamed at them until his bald head turned red.


“I suppose it’s just me and you,” Lora said. She slid off her stool.
She sauntered over to the easel.
Her intrepid hand brushed the back of Mandri’s arm.
“Let me see what my uncle has—OH my GOD!” 
Her portrait looked like her face had been painted onto an apple, and then that apple was run over by a cartwheel. She recoiled in horror.
“It could be better,” Mandri said.
“It’s terrifying!” She squeezed Mandri’s scrawny shoulders as she hid herself behind him, pressing herself against him in genuine fright.
“I think it’s the hands,” Mandri said. He stepped forward to make a play at correcting them.
Lora sidled forward with him, stifling a laugh. “He’s turned me into a demon!” she exclaimed. “It’s not only the hands…though they do look like claws.” She grabbed Mandri’s own hand and gave it a squeeze. “Artists need strong and dexterous hands, don’t they?” 
“Uh…no…” Mandri stammered. “Well, yes, but, uh… I don’t have any, uh, dexterity. I actually haven’t painted anything in years.”
“Then what have you been doing up on that little farm of yours?”
“Farming.”
“I doubt that,” Lora teased. “You don’t look like you’ve farmed a day in your life.”
“Are you calling me a liar?”
“I’ve seen your paintings, Mandri. They’re good! I like them.”
“I don’t.”


“Is that why won’t you paint?”
Mandri tore himself away from her grip. He busied himself with Vigram’s mess, as there was enough untouched pigment left on the palette for at least four more of these awkward lessons. He moved to scrape a slap of yellow back into its little clay jar.
“You’re a very disturbed boy,” Lora said. “I can’t think of anyone else who worked so hard to acquire a skill and then refuse to use it.”
“It’s too expensive,” Mandri said instinctively. “Look at all this pigment. The canvas. It would take me weeks to afford all of this. Your uncle makes this in half a finger.”
“You could make that much too if you worked for him.”
“I do not want to do that.”
Lora shrugged, then picked up Vigram’s dirty brush and twirled its horse-hair head through the muddy colors of the palette. “You know, Vigram told me that he doesn’t actually care about learning to paint. The only reason he pays for these lessons is because you would starve if he didn’t. He likes you. He thinks that we would be a good match.” She batted her eyes and tried to catch him staring.
“Lora, I don’t need his charity.”
“Mandri, I’ve seen your farm.”
“When?”
“Everyone’s seen your farm. It’s impossible to miss because it is not a farm, it is a desert.”
“It’s a farm to me.”
Lora rolled the handle of the paintbrush in between her fingers. She moved into kissing distance. Her sly, expectant look pricked Mandri’s skin. “I’m a woman, Mandri. What’s your problem?”
“Confusion, generally.”
“How is this confusing?” She grabbed Mandri’s arm and pulled him into a squeeze. She caught his eyes, forced him to confront her lilac scent, her pounding heat, her smooth skin, her honeyed, aching lips.
“Lora…” Mandri muttered. He knew he had to be very careful here. “You’re a beautiful girl, but—”
“But what? You go in for men?”
“I don’t know you.”
“Mandri, look at me. I would be good for you.”
“How could you possibly know that?”
Lora chuckled. “Because I can tell you’ve never had fun before.” Her inviting smile widened. Her warmth overwhelmed. The soft shadows of the vines overhead framed her hazel eyes with the most magnificent golden light—a magical vesture indeed.
She leaned in.
Her parted lips paused just shy of his cheek.
Her breath prickled his skin.
And now he was on the precipice. His hand gripped her waist. Her brown robe was so very thin, and the heat of her, intense. Coupled with the curve of her figure, it was almost enough to make him forget that this was a total stranger who had no business being interested in him, and more than that, this was not what he was here for.
His gaze poked through the trellis behind Lora’s flower-studded curls. In between the diamond gaps of wood and leafy vine, he glimpsed that giant yellow-headed ox hurtling towards them both. The lower half of its body was covered in swinging leeches.
“Watch out!” he shouted as he shoved Lora out of the way. He dove aside himself. The old ox, sagging spine and all, rammed right through the trellis, sending a shower of wood splinters onto the patio. Dewlap flapping, it crashed into Vigram’s easel. Several foot long leeches flew through the air.
“MRRRRUUUUUUUUUU!”
The ox’s wheeling hooves scratched and slipped on the brick. After a moment of chaos, it steadied itself, legs wide like a water bug, and then it bucked into the opposite side of the trellis. It broke through, scraped a few more leeches off its haunches, then disappeared into the marshgrass.


Vigram stormed back out onto the patio. Head red and sweaty, panting. His eyes darted between Lora, who lay sprawled on the floor, Mandri, who was much the same, and his abandoned painting, which lay face down on the dirty brick. Two little leeches wiggled on top of the frame. Then his wild gaze seized upon the ox’s shaved rump as it bounced through the grass. His jaw dropped. His eyes widened. He transcended the bounds of anger, peaking into pure amazement, like he’d just heard an upstart beggar claim to be god, only to find out, the rascal was.
“I see Fate!” Vigram declared.
Mandri brushed the dirt off the skirt of his robe. He stood up. “I’m leaving,” he said firmly. He was still bewildered by Lora’s advance, and the ox’s intrusion, and now, Vigram’s rapturous mood. Against all this, he dared to hold out his hand, upturned—“If you could pay me my money, that would be great.”
Vigram’s upper lip curled. “Money? At a time like this? Can’t you see how you’ve been brushed by Fate? You and she were to kiss, and in the decisive moment, the ox interrupted. Fate on four legs! Here I was, calling for the beast to be caged!”
“Oh, calm yourself,” Lora said. All the girlish charm in her voice had vanished. &quot;Not every interruption is a scheme of Fate.” Stern and measured, she stood up and fixed her hair. She smiled at Mandri’s demanding hand like it was cute. “He is not as empty inside as you claimed,” she said. “Not only did he succumb to temptation, but he desired to protect himself from injury. He desires life.”
“What, because he dodged the ox’s charge? I addressed this before—we should not mistake any acts of self-preservation for vitality. He is a broken young man, I assure you.”
“I’m sorry,” Mandri interjected, still holding out his hand. “What is happening here?”
Vigram chuckled, then stroked his beard. “She was a test to determine how tethered you were to your flesh.”
“You were seduced,” Lora added.
“Seduced?” Mandri scoffed. “Is that what you were trying to do? I didn’t notice. You should try stuffing your tits.”
“You see!?” Vigram exclaimed. “The moment he learns your interest in him was a ruse, he moves to mock you. He cannot bear the thought that he was taken advantage of, made a fool. See the way he stands. I should say that the boy is nothing more than a thin veneer lidding a black pit of shame. I say, he is exactly what we need! Look at him! He is empty!”
“He is common,” Lora intoned. 
“But the ox! You never truly kissed!”
Lora gave Vigram a considerate frown. “I suppose it could be called an auspicious moment.” She eyed Mandri as though appraising a slave. “I shall query his Fate again.” She stomped back across the patio, then sort of drifted into the darkness of Vigram’s house.
Mandri’s legs were sweating fiercely now. He knew he should run, if only because these people were taking up far too much of his time with some nonsense. “Please,” he said. “Just pay me what I’m owed, and I’ll be on my way.”
“Mandri,” Vigram said, “What precisely do you believe you are owed?”
“A quarter silver string?”
“You are owed quite a bit more than that.”
Lora’s commanding voice boomed out of the house. “Tomorrow!” she said. “If you recall, I am required elsewhere to-day!”
“Ah,” Vigram said. He returned his attention to Mandri. “Tomorrow, then.” He reached into his robe’s pocket. He pulled out a quarter string of silver, then dropped it into Mandri’s cupped hand. “I want to see you here tomorrow morning. I can promise you an opportunity which will give you everything that you ever wanted. I should add, you will not have to work for it.”
“Sure,” Mandri mumbled as he fingered the short stack of silver coins. He did not count them, just felt their weight. He wasn’t interested in lingering any longer than he needed to. He wasn’t interested in returning any time soon. Definitely not tomorrow. He had plans for tomorrow.
He hurried down the patio’s steps, refusing to return Vigram’s parting nod.
“Look happier!” Vigram called. “You should be excited!”
Mandri gave a polite smile over his shoulder, then hurried down Vigram’s lane, which was the most terrifying lane because it was sandwiched between two leech lakes.
Mandri kept to the right. The lake on the left held leeches bigger than his arm. Instead of stalls penning in live animals, its banks were lined with bones. The lake’s center was a constant swirl as hundreds of leeches swarmed just under the water’s surface, bunch-crawling like caterpillars, slithering like eels, desperate to get in a taste of whatever corpse had bothered to drop in. Mandri grimaced at the sight of it. Then, suddenly, a round shape popped out of that writhing mass like a buoy. It rotated around, revealing itself to be a severed head, somewhat fresh, a human head, complete with close cropped hair, an upturned nose, and an open mouth which could not scream. A leech latched onto its cheek, and as soon as that had come, it sank.
“Nope!” Mandri said to himself. “Nope, nope, nope,” he repeated as he darted towards Vigram’s gate. Once onto the road, he quickened his pace. He didn’t feel out of reach until he crested the big hill that overlooked Vigram’s estate. Only then did he risk a look back.
Vigram, still on his patio, stared straight through the distance, right at Mandri. The burly man cupped his voice and shouted, “Fate, boy! Fate!”
Mandri pretended not to hear.
He walked all the way to Geem’s and used his pay to buy half a bushel of burgeons. They were down-valley burgeons, decent stuff, but the waxy capsule leaves were liable to split. The insides would dry out before he could get to them in the ways that he wanted to. But, that was all Geem had, so he bought them.
He continued along the road, stomping his way through the muddy wheel-ruts, slapping away the flies, humming nursery rhymes, trying anything to distract himself from overthinking the sight of that severed head. Because it was only a severed head. That’s all it was! It might have never belonged to a body, or been a person. There’s no way of knowing, really. Anything was possible. Besides, a rich man like Vigram was sure to accumulate enemies alongside the rest of his possessions. What was one little head?
Mandri thought about his barren farm.
He thought about relaxing under his tree.
He thought about sipping a milky hooch spiked through with burgeon juice.
He would like to escape into that hooch. It would be bliss.</Text>
